                "Economic Value to the Customer (EVC) is the maximum price a rational business buyer would be willing to pay for an offering, given their existing alternative options. EVC is formulated across three structural components: Reference Value, Positive Differentiation Value, and Negative Differentiation Value: EVC = Reference Value + Positive Differentiation Value - Negative Differentiation Value. The Reference Value is the price of the customer's next-best competitive alternative (the status quo solution). Positive Differentiation Value represents the quantified monetary value of superior benefits delivered by your offering: incremental revenue generated, operational labor hours saved, scrap material eliminated, or regulatory compliance risk mitigated. Negative Differentiation Value accounts for any unique disadvantages: higher switching friction, software retraining costs, or ancillary tooling requirements.",
                "EVC establishes the absolute theoretical ceiling of willingness-to-pay (WTP). A sophisticated enterprise pricing team never prices at the full EVC; pricing at 100% of EVC leaves zero incentive for the customer to switch vendors. Instead, the firm implements Value Sharing: dividing the differentiation value between the customer and the enterprise. For example, if a SaaS platform delivers $1,000,000 in net operational cost savings annually over a competitor (Reference Value: $200,000), the firm might price at $400,000—capturing $200,000 in incremental gross profit while leaving $600,000 in customer surplus, creating an undeniable, ROI-backed sales proposition.",
                "To measure consumer willingness-to-pay for consumer products and new market innovations where explicit B2B ROI calculations are unfeasible, marketing scientists deploy the Van Westendorp Price Sensitivity Meter (PSM). Formulated in 1976 by Dutch economist Peter van Westendorp, the PSM surveys target consumers using four standardized psychological questions: 1. At what price would you consider the product to be so inexpensive that you would question its quality and not buy it? (Too Cheap); 2. At what price would you consider the product to be a bargain—a great buy for the money? (Cheap / Bargain); 3. At what price would you consider the product to be getting expensive, but you would still consider buying it? (Expensive); 4. At what price would you consider the product to be so expensive that you would not consider buying it? (Too Expensive).",
                "By plotting the cumulative frequency distributions of these four curves against price, the PSM identifies four critical market pricing boundaries at their intersections: The Point of Marginal Cheapness (PMC: intersection of Too Cheap and Expensive; prices below PMC suffer severe quality skepticism and brand damage); The Point of Marginal Expensiveness (PME: intersection of Cheap and Too Expensive; prices above PME encounter massive customer refusal); The Indifference Price Point (IPP: intersection of Cheap and Expensive; the median price where equal numbers of consumers view the product as cheap versus expensive); and The Optimal Price Point (OPP: intersection of Too Cheap and Too Expensive; the price point that minimizes consumer purchase resistance and maximizes sales conversion velocity).",
                "The range between PMC and PME defines the Range of Acceptable Pricing (RAP). Operating within the RAP ensures that the enterprise captures maximum revenue without falling into the 'suspiciously cheap' discount trap or the 'prohibitively expensive' demand cliff."
